<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Agincourt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1415</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Agincourt · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Agincourt was fought on 25 October 1415 in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where Henry V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s small, exhausted army, made up largely of longbowmen, defeated a much larger French force. Heavy rain and a narrow muddy field helped the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and many leading French nobles, among them Duke Anthony of Brabant, were killed. The victory opened the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s conquest of Normandy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt.jpg" alt="Battle of Agincourt" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_von_Azincourt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
